/*!
    \page qtbridges-swift-index.html
    \title Qt Bridges - Swift
    \brief How to get started with Qt Bridges using Swift.

    \section1 Getting started
    Qt Bridges allows writing QML applications with Swift without needing to write
    C++ code. It removes typical Qt boilerplate and provides a Swift-native feel.

    \section2 Installation
    \section3 Requirements
    \list
    \li macOS 15 or later
    \li Xcode 16 or later
    \li Swift 6.1 or later
    \endlist

    \section3 Overview
    Qt Bridges is distributed as Swift Package Manager (SwiftPM) package, which
    includes bundled prebuilt Qt binaries. Qt itself is listed as a dependecy of
    Qt Bridges, so SwiftPM will fetch it automatically.

    There are two ways to add Qt Bridges to a project:

    \section4 Add via Xcode
    \list
    \li In Xcode, select \e File -> \e{Add Package Dependencies}.
    \li Enter the Qt Bridges package URL.
    \li Xcode will automatically resolve and download Qt Bridges and its dependencies.
    \li Link the Qt Bridges product to the app target.
    \li Enable Swift-C++ interoperability:
        \list
        \li Go to the target's \e{Build Settings}.
        \li Switch the filter to \e All + \e Combined.
        \li Search for \e Interoperability.
        \li Under \e{Swift Compiler - Language}, set \e{C++ and Objective-C Interoperability}
        mode to \e{C++/Objective-C++}.
        \endlist
    \endlist

    \section4 Add via Package.swift
    \list
    \li Specify the Qt Bridges package URL in the dependencies section.
    \li Link the Qt Bridges product to the app target.
    \li Enable Swift-C++ interoperability in \e swiftSettings with \e .interoperabilityMode(.Cxx) mode.
    \endlist
    \badcode
        dependencies: [
            .package(url: "<QtBridges-repo-url>", from: "")
        ],
        targets: [
            .target(
                name: "MyApp",
                dependencies: [
                    .product(name: "QtBridges", package: "QtBridges")
                ],
                swiftSettings: [
                    .interoperabilityMode(.Cxx)
                ]
            )
        ]
    \endcode

    \section2 Setting up development environment
    \list
    \li Xcode / Swift toolchain: Swift 6.x with C++ interoperability enabled (Xcode 16+).
    \li Ensure Qt Bridges is linked to the app target.
    \endlist

    \section2 Building the Qt Bridges package
    From the Qt Bridges repository root, run
    \badcode
    swift build -c debug
    \endcode
    or
    \badcode
    swift build -c release
    \endcode

    SwiftPM will automatically fetch the binary Qt dependency declared by Qt Bridges.

    \section2 Running
    Example application that uses Qt Bridges can be run either from command line or
    directly in Xcode.

    \section3 Command line
    From the example root, run:
    \badcode
    swift run
    \endcode

    \section3 Xcode
    \list
    \li Open the repository folder in Xcode and select Package.swift
    \li Press the Run button
    \endlist
    In both cases, SwiftPM will automatically fetch the Qt binary dependency declared by
    Qt Bridges on the first build.

    \section1 Tutorials

    \section2 Running the Example Minimal Application
    The example app included with Qt Bridges is already set up with its own Package.swift.
    You can run it either directly in Xcode or from the command line.

    \section3 Running via Xcode
    \list
    \li Locate the example project folder.
    \li Double-click on Package.swift — Xcode will open the project automatically.
    \li In Xcode, select the example app target.
    \li Press the Run button.
    \endlist

    \section3 Running via Command Line
    \list
    \li Open Terminal and navigate to the example project root.
    \li Run:
    \badcode
    swift run
    \endcode
    \li SwiftPM will build the project and launch the app.
    \endlist

    In both cases, SwiftPM will automatically fetch and build the Qt binary dependency on the first run.
*/
